CARDS = [
# ---------- Node 21 ----------
("node-21", "Node 21 · Files and inodes", "What is Unix's definition of a file, and why was it radical?", "A finite sequence of bytes, and nothing more — no records, no line structure, no type the system knows. Contemporary systems like OS/360 had record-oriented files whose format the system enforced. Throwing that away is what makes any program's output usable as any other's input, and it is the root of the pipe-based toolchain."),
("node-21", "Node 21 · Files and inodes", "What is an inode, and what is not in it?", "A fixed-size on-disk record holding the file's type, permissions, owner, size, timestamps, link count and data block pointers. The <em>name</em> is not in it — names live in directories."),
("node-21", "Node 21 · Files and inodes", "What is a directory?", "A file whose contents are a list of name-to-inode-number pairs. Nothing more."),
("node-21", "Node 21 · Files and inodes", "Why is deleting called <code>unlink</code>?", "It removes a directory entry and decrements the inode's link count. The data is freed only when the count reaches zero <em>and</em> no process still holds the file open."),
("node-21", "Node 21 · Files and inodes", "You deleted a 40 GB log and <code>df</code> still shows the disk full. Why, and what do you do?", "A running process still has it open, so the link count reached zero but the reference count did not. <code>lsof | grep deleted</code> finds the holder; restart or signal it, or truncate the file rather than deleting it next time."),
("node-21", "Node 21 · Files and inodes", "Why is write-temp-then-rename the standard safe-write idiom?", "<code>rename()</code> is atomic with respect to readers: they see either the whole old file or the whole new one, never a partial write. It works because an open file has no name — a reader holding the old inode is unaffected."),
("node-21", "Node 21 · Files and inodes", "Contrast hard links and symbolic links.", "A hard link is another directory entry for the same inode — equally the file, sharing permissions, unable to cross filesystems or point at directories (cycles). A symlink is a tiny file whose contents are a path string, resolved at every use — so it can cross filesystems, can dangle, and adds a resolution step."),
("node-21", "Node 21 · Files and inodes", "How can a disk with free space refuse writes with <code>ENOSPC</code>?", "Inode exhaustion. Traditional filesystems allocate a fixed inode table at format time, so millions of tiny files can use them all. <code>df -h</code> shows space free; <code>df -i</code> shows the truth."),
("node-21", "Node 21 · Files and inodes", "Name four things that are files but are not files.", "Devices (<code>/dev/null</code>, <code>/dev/zero</code>, <code>/dev/urandom</code>, <code>/dev/sda</code>); pipes and sockets; kernel state (<code>/proc</code>, <code>/sys</code> — reading executes kernel code that generates the content); and terminals. The payoff is that every tool written for files works on all of it."),
("node-21", "Node 21 · Files and inodes", "What is the VFS and what does it make possible?", "A kernel layer defining operations (look up, read, write, create) that every filesystem implements — introduced by Sun in 1985 for NFS. It is why ext4, XFS, NFS, tmpfs, overlayfs, procfs and FUSE all live in one tree, and why <code>mount</code> can attach any filesystem at any point."),
("node-21", "Node 21 · Files and inodes", "What is a bind mount, and why does it hide things?", "The same inodes appearing at a second location in the tree — no copy, no sync, literally the same files. A mount replaces what a path resolves to, so bind-mounting your source over <code>/app</code> hides the image's <code>/app/node_modules</code>; an anonymous volume at that deeper path shadows the first mount, which is why that trick works."),
("node-21", "Node 21 · Files and inodes", "Why does moving a file from <code>/tmp</code> to a volume behave differently from moving within it?", "<code>rename</code> across filesystems fails with <code>EXDEV</code>, so the library falls back to copy-then-delete, which is not atomic. Upload handling should write the temporary file into the destination filesystem."),
("node-21", "Node 21 · Files and inodes", "Why does a dev server on a big repo fail with <code>ENOSPC</code> when there is plenty of disk?", "<code>inotify</code> watch limits — <code>fs.inotify.max_user_watches</code>, historically 8192. It is one of the most misleading error codes in Linux, since no space is involved."),
("node-21", "Node 21 · Files and inodes", "What problem is pnpm solving, in this node's terms?", "Hundreds of thousands of small files are slow to create, slow to traverse, slow to send as build context, and inode-hungry. pnpm keeps one content-addressed store and hard-links into each project, so a file exists once on disk with many names; Yarn PnP eliminates the directory entirely."),
# ---------- Node 22 ----------
("node-22", "Node 22 · Descriptors and pipes", "Describe the three tables behind a file descriptor.", "The per-process descriptor table (the small integer indexes it, and entries hold a pointer plus close-on-exec); the system-wide open file table, where each <em>open file description</em> holds the offset, access mode and status flags; and the inode table. Collapsing these to one is the source of every confusion about descriptors."),
("node-22", "Node 22 · Descriptors and pipes", "Why does opening a file twice give independent offsets while <code>fork</code> gives a shared one?", "Two <code>open</code> calls create two open file descriptions. <code>dup</code> and <code>fork</code> create two descriptors pointing at <em>one</em> description — so a parent and child writing to inherited stdout interleave correctly rather than overwriting each other. That is the reason for the design."),
("node-22", "Node 22 · Descriptors and pipes", "Why are stdout and stderr separate descriptors?", "So results and complaints can go to different places — which is what makes <code>./build &gt; out.log 2&gt; err.log</code> possible and why error messages still reach your terminal when output is piped."),
("node-22", "Node 22 · Descriptors and pipes", "Which one-line specification makes shell redirection possible?", "<code>open</code> always returns the lowest-numbered unused descriptor. Close descriptor 1, then open the file: it becomes descriptor 1."),
("node-22", "Node 22 · Descriptors and pipes", "Derive <code>ls &gt; out.txt</code>.", "<code>fork</code>; in the child, <code>dup2</code> the opened file onto descriptor 1 (or close 1 then open); <code>execve</code> <code>/bin/ls</code>, which keeps descriptors. <code>ls</code> writes to 1 as always, knowing nothing. Redirection is descriptor-table rearrangement in the gap between fork and exec."),
("node-22", "Node 22 · Descriptors and pipes", "What are a pipe's three blocking behaviours, and what does each give you?", "Writing to a full pipe blocks — that is backpressure built into the primitive. Reading an empty one blocks until data or until <em>all</em> write ends close, giving end-of-file. Writing with no readers raises <code>SIGPIPE</code>, which is why <code>yes | head -5</code> terminates: producers die when consumers stop caring."),
("node-22", "Node 22 · Descriptors and pipes", "Why does a shell script sometimes hang waiting on a pipe that should have ended?", "Some process still holds a write end open — often an inherited descriptor a subshell forgot to close — so the reader never sees EOF."),
("node-22", "Node 22 · Descriptors and pipes", "Derive <code>cmd1 | cmd2</code>.", "Create a pipe; fork twice; in the first child <code>dup2(fds[1], 1)</code> and close both original ends; in the second <code>dup2(fds[0], 0)</code> and close both; exec both. Two unmodified programs connected using nothing but the descriptor table."),
("node-22", "Node 22 · Descriptors and pipes", "What does the <code>stdio</code> option in <code>child_process</code> actually specify?", "The child's descriptor table. <code>'pipe'</code> creates a pipe and gives you a stream; <code>'inherit'</code> passes your own descriptor through so the child writes directly to your terminal or log with no relaying; <code>'ignore'</code> opens <code>/dev/null</code>; and <code>'ipc'</code> as a fourth entry is the message channel <code>child_process.fork()</code> uses."),
("node-22", "Node 22 · Descriptors and pipes", "Explain the classic subprocess deadlock and its two fixes.", "The child produces 10 MB on stdout, the parent waits for exit before reading; the pipe fills at 64 KB, the child blocks writing, the parent blocks waiting. It is a bounded-queue deadlock. Fixes: consume output continuously (<code>spawn</code> with streams), or accept <code>exec</code>'s <code>maxBuffer</code> limit and its <code>ENOBUFS</code>."),
("node-22", "Node 22 · Descriptors and pipes", "Why does the 12-factor rule about logging to stdout matter mechanically?", "Docker gives PID 1 a pipe for stdout and stderr and reads from it. Writing to a file inside the container bypasses all of it, so <code>docker logs</code> and every shipper see nothing. It also means a stalled log consumer fills the pipe and your application blocks on <code>console.log</code>."),
("node-22", "Node 22 · Descriptors and pipes", "What is close-on-exec and why does it matter for security?", "Without <code>FD_CLOEXEC</code>, every descriptor you hold is inherited by every subprocess — including database connections, key files, and listening sockets, so a subprocess can read from an already-authenticated socket. Modern APIs default to it, which is why deliberately passing a descriptor (cluster, systemd socket activation) requires clearing it."),
# ---------- Node 23 ----------
("node-23", "Node 23 · Storage and fsync", "Give the two numbers that shape database design.", "A hard disk random read is a seek (4–10 ms) plus half a rotation (4.2 ms at 7200 rpm) — around 8–12 ms. Sequential transfer once positioned is 100–200 MB/s. The ratio is roughly 100 to 1."),
("node-23", "Node 23 · Storage and fsync", "Name three database designs that exist because of that ratio.", "B-trees, to keep seeks logarithmic; write-ahead logs, to convert random writes into sequential ones; and the planner's preference for a sequential scan past a certain selectivity, encoded as <code>random_page_cost</code> 4.0 against <code>seq_page_cost</code> 1.0 — which is why lowering it to ~1.1 on SSD is standard and changes plans."),
("node-23", "Node 23 · Storage and fsync", "Why does an SSD contain a translation layer?", "Flash is read and written in 4–16 KB pages but erased only in multi-megabyte blocks, and pages cannot be overwritten in place. So the Flash Translation Layer maps logical addresses to physical pages, writing to fresh pages and garbage-collecting stale blocks in the background."),
("node-23", "Node 23 · Storage and fsync", "Name three consequences of the FTL you can observe.", "Write amplification (writing 4 KB causes far more flash traffic); latency spikes when garbage collection collides with your writes, so p99 is much worse than the average; and the need for <code>TRIM</code>, without which a full-then-emptied SSD stays slow."),
("node-23", "Node 23 · Storage and fsync", "State the latency hierarchy from L1 to a cross-continent round trip.", "L1 ~1 ns; L2 ~4 ns; DRAM ~80 ns; NVMe ~20–80 µs; SATA SSD ~100 µs; same-datacentre network ~100–500 µs; disk seek ~5–10 ms; cross-continent ~100 ms."),
("node-23", "Node 23 · Storage and fsync", "Why is the modern I/O scheduler for an NVMe device <code>none</code>?", "The elevator algorithm existed to avoid pathological head movement on spinning disks. NVMe devices have thousands of deep queues and reorder better internally than the kernel can."),
("node-23", "Node 23 · Storage and fsync", "Where are your bytes after <code>write()</code> returns, and what survives what?", "In the kernel page cache: survives a process crash including <code>SIGKILL</code>, lost on power failure or panic. Before that they may still be in your language's buffer, lost on a process crash. After writeback they are in the device's volatile write cache, still lost on power failure. Only the medium survives everything."),
("node-23", "Node 23 · Storage and fsync", "What does <code>fsync</code> guarantee, and what does <code>fdatasync</code> skip?", "It writes back all dirty pages of that file and issues a cache-flush to the device, returning only when the device says the data is durable. <code>fdatasync</code> may skip metadata that does not affect reading the data back, and is what databases usually use."),
("node-23", "Node 23 · Storage and fsync", "Why is <code>fsync</code> the most important number in database performance?", "An in-memory row insert is a microsecond; a committed durable insert is bounded below by one <code>fsync</code> — milliseconds on a disk, tens to hundreds of microseconds on a consumer SSD, near zero on enterprise hardware with power-loss protection. Every durability setting is a decision about how many to do."),
("node-23", "Node 23 · Storage and fsync", "What was fsyncgate and what did Postgres change?", "In 2018 it emerged that on Linux, when writeback fails, the error is reported to the next <code>fsync</code> caller and the pages are then marked clean — so a retry returns success while the data is gone. Postgres 12 now panics on <code>fsync</code> failure and recovers from the WAL. The general lesson: an I/O error is not retryable unless you know what the layer beneath did with the failed data."),
("node-23", "Node 23 · Storage and fsync", "What is a torn page, and what is Postgres's defence?", "A power cut partway through writing an 8 KB page leaves half new and half old, because hardware only guarantees atomicity at sector granularity. <code>full_page_writes</code> writes the entire page image into the WAL on its first modification after each checkpoint — roughly doubling WAL volume, and the reason disabling it is tempting and wrong."),
("node-23", "Node 23 · Storage and fsync", "Derive the write-ahead log from first principles.", "Modifying a row means a random write, possibly across several pages, which cannot be made atomic. So append a description of the change to a sequential log, <code>fsync</code> it, and report the commit; update the data pages lazily later; replay the log after a crash. One random write becomes one sequential append plus one <code>fsync</code>."),
("node-23", "Node 23 · Storage and fsync", "What does <code>synchronous_commit = off</code> actually risk?", "Not corruption — only the loss of the last fraction of a second of committed transactions. A reasonable trade for analytics ingestion or event logging and a bad one for payments. Also note group commit: concurrent commits share one <code>fsync</code>, so throughput scales far better than single-transaction latency suggests."),
("node-23", "Node 23 · Storage and fsync", "What do <code>innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit</code> 1, 2 and 0 mean?", "1 flushes per commit (durable, default); 2 writes to the page cache per commit and flushes each second, so it survives a process crash but not a power cut; 0 does neither."),
("node-23", "Node 23 · Storage and fsync", "Which three <code>iostat</code> and <code>top</code> numbers diagnose storage, and what does each mean?", "<code>await</code> — average request latency, the number that matters. <code>aqu-sz</code> — queue depth; high <code>await</code> with a deep queue means saturation. <code>%util</code> — misleading on parallel-queue devices. And <code>wa</code> in <code>top</code>: high <code>wa</code> with low user CPU means the machine is storage-bound."),
("node-23", "Node 23 · Storage and fsync", "Why did a database that was fast for three weeks become permanently slow?", "Exhausted burst credits on a burstable cloud volume (or a burstable instance). It drops to a baseline that can be a fraction of what you tested against, and no query tuning fixes it."),
("node-23", "Node 23 · Storage and fsync", "Why must a database never run on a container's writable layer?", "Every first write to a file pays a full copy-up through the overlay driver. Volumes go to the host filesystem directly. It is a mechanical cost, not just a persistence concern."),
# ---------- Node 24 ----------
("node-24", "Node 24 · Filesystems", "What does a filesystem lay out on a device?", "A superblock (size, block size, where everything is, a clean/dirty flag); allocation structures for free blocks and inodes; the inode table; and the data blocks."),
("node-24", "Node 24 · Filesystems", "Why did extents replace indirect blocks?", "Indirect blocks describe a large file with hundreds of thousands of pointers. An extent is a start block plus a length, so a contiguous 1 GB file needs a handful of records. It is a large part of why ext4 outperforms ext3 on big files."),
("node-24", "Node 24 · Filesystems", "Why is <code>fsck</code> unacceptable on a server, and what replaced it?", "Scanning a multi-terabyte filesystem takes hours during which the machine is down. Journaling replaced it: write a description of the intended updates to a journal and flush, perform them, mark the entry complete, and after a crash replay only the incomplete entries — bounded work in seconds."),
("node-24", "Node 24 · Filesystems", "What do ext4's three data modes guarantee?", "<code>data=journal</code>: data and metadata both journaled, safest, everything written twice. <code>data=ordered</code> (default): metadata journaled, but data blocks forced to disk before the metadata referencing them, so you never see another file's old contents. <code>data=writeback</code>: no ordering, fastest, and after a crash a file can contain arbitrary stale bytes."),
("node-24", "Node 24 · Filesystems", "Why can a crash leave a file that exists, has the right size, and is full of zeros?", "ext4 journals metadata, not data, and uses delayed allocation. It is documented behaviour of <code>data=ordered</code>, not a bug — which is why applications that care must <code>fsync</code>."),
("node-24", "Node 24 · Filesystems", "Write a genuinely safe file update.", "Write the temp file; <code>fsync</code> the temp file; <code>rename</code> over the target; <code>fsync</code> the containing directory. The rename alone gives atomicity for readers but not durability against power loss."),
("node-24", "Node 24 · Filesystems", "What is tmpfs and what is its container trap?", "A filesystem in RAM backed by the page cache and swap — <code>/dev/shm</code>, much of <code>/run</code>, Docker's <code>--tmpfs</code>, and Kubernetes' memory-medium <code>emptyDir</code>. Its contents count against your cgroup memory limit, so writing a few gigabytes into one gets the container OOMKilled for reasons the error does not explain."),
("node-24", "Node 24 · Filesystems", "Why should a database never live on NFS?", "Network filesystems break local assumptions: locking is advisory and unreliable, <code>fsync</code> semantics vary, latency is milliseconds, and an unresponsive server puts your process into unkillable <code>D</code> state."),
("node-24", "Node 24 · Filesystems", "Describe overlayfs's three operations.", "Read: search the upper layer, then each lower layer in order, first hit wins. Write to a lower-layer file: <em>copy-up</em> the entire file into the upper layer first, then modify. Delete a lower-layer file: create a whiteout marker in the upper layer to hide it."),
("node-24", "Node 24 · Filesystems", "Explain layer sharing and why ten containers from one image are cheap.", "The lower directories are one set of files on disk, mounted read-only into every container; each container adds only a thin writable upper layer. It is also why a common base image across services is a real saving."),
("node-24", "Node 24 · Filesystems", "Why does <code>RUN rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*</code> in a separate instruction not shrink the image?", "The earlier layer still contains the files; the later layer only adds whiteouts. This is also how credentials leak: a secret deleted in a later instruction is still extractable from the layer where it was added. Hence multi-stage builds and BuildKit <code>--mount=type=secret</code>."),
("node-24", "Node 24 · Filesystems", "Why is <code>COPY package*.json</code> then <code>RUN npm ci</code> then <code>COPY . .</code> not a style convention?", "A layer's cache is invalidated when its inputs change, and every layer after it is then rebuilt. This order puts the slow, rarely-invalidated step above the fast, always-invalidated one. Reversed, dependencies reinstall on every source change."),
("node-24", "Node 24 · Filesystems", "Why is a bind mount slow on Docker Desktop?", "Linux containers there run inside a virtual machine, so the mount crosses from the host filesystem into the VM through a protocol (osxfs, then gRPC-FUSE, now VirtioFS). Every <code>stat</code> crosses it, and a bundler statting a hundred thousand files becomes an order of magnitude slower. Keep <code>node_modules</code> in a named volume, and on Windows work inside the WSL2 filesystem rather than <code>/mnt/c</code>."),
("node-24", "Node 24 · Filesystems", "Why does <code>import Button from './button'</code> work on a Mac and fail in CI?", "APFS is case-insensitive by default; ext4 is case-sensitive. It is one of the most common works-on-my-machine failures in JavaScript projects and it is one line of filesystem behaviour."),
]
